The function $ g(x) = \frac{\sin(1/x)}{1/x} $ simplifies to $ g(x) = x \sin(1/x) $, which is continuous on $ (0, 1] $. As $ x \to 0^+ $, $ g(x) \to 0 $, allowing us to extend $ g(x) $ continuously to $ [0, 1] $ by defining $ g(0) = 0 $. By the Weierstrass Approximation Theorem, any continuous function on a closed interval can be uniformly approximated by polynomials. Since the extended function is continuous on $ [0, 1] $, there exists a sequence of polynomials converging uniformly to it on $ [0, 1] $, and hence also on $ (0, 1] $.

**Final Answer**
\boxed{Yes}

